   117	  `https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0305088`
   118	
   119	## 3. Every component of `B` is exactly a component of `A_F`
   120	
   121	The promoted block theorem gives
   122	
   123	```text
   124	B=g2(R)_red subset A_F.                                (3.1)
   125	```
   126	
   127	This is enough for equality componentwise, not merely containment.  Since
   128	`R` is a nonempty pure curve and `g2` is finite, every irreducible component
   129	of `B` is a one-dimensional irreducible closed subset.  The nonproper-value
   130	set `A_F`, if nonempty, is a plane curve.  An irreducible component `B_i` is
   131	contained in some irreducible component `A_j` of `A_F`.  Both are closed
   132	irreducible curves, so equality of dimensions forces
   133	
   134	```text
   135	B_i=A_j.                                               (3.2)
   136	```
   137	
   138	This does not say that `B=A_F`; components caused only by the first-leg
   139	nonproperness may remain outside `B`.
   140	
   141	By Theorem 1, each `B_i` is the image of a nonconstant polynomial map from
   142	`A1`.  Its normalization is therefore `A1`.  One quick proof is to lift the
   143	map to the normalization and extend it to a nonconstant morphism from `P1`
   144	to the smooth projective completion.  The latter has genus zero.  Every
   145	puncture of the normalization must have all its preimages at the single
   146	source point `infinity`; surjectivity of the completed curve map permits at
   147	most one puncture, and affineness permits at least one.  Thus exactly one
   148	point is deleted from `P1`.
   149	
   150	## 4. The cubic source-to-target bridge
   151	
   152	Fix `z in B(C)`.  The finite-flat fibre `Y_z` has scheme length three.  At a
   153	closed point `y` of this fibre, flatness implies:
   154	
   155	```text
   156	g2 is etale at y  iff  the local geometric fibre factor at y is reduced,
   157	                        equivalently it has length one over C.
   158	```
   159	
   160	Thus every point of `R` in the fibre has local fibre length at least two.
   161	There cannot be two such points, because their lengths would sum to at least
   162	four.  There is at least one because `z` lies in `g2(R)`.  Hence every closed
   163	fibre of
   164	
   165	```text
   166	g2|R : R -> B
   167	```
   168	
   169	contains exactly one point.  After reduction the restriction is finite and
   170	bijective on complex points.  A finite complex-algebraic morphism is proper
   171	on analytifications, and a proper continuous bijection between locally
   172	compact Hausdorff spaces is a homeomorphism.  Therefore
   173	
   174	```text
   175	R(C) homeomorphic to B(C),
   176	e_c(R)=e_c(B),                  b0(R)=b0(B).            (4.1)
   177	```
   178	
   179	It also follows in characteristic zero that corresponding irreducible
   180	components are birational.  Their normalizations agree, so every component
   181	of `R` has normalization `A1` by Section 3.
   182	
   183	This is exactly where degree three enters.  At degree four the fibre
   184	partition `(2,2)` can have two source ramification points over one target
   185	point.  Then a finite residue map can identify branches or components and
   186	create target nodes/cycles.  No statement in this report transfers to that
   187	case.
   188	
   189	## 5. Forest Euler lemma on the source
   190	
   191	Choose a projective compactification of `Y` and resolve the pair consisting
   192	of the closure of `R` and the divisor at infinity.  This is a smooth
   193	projective SNC completion of `U=Y minus R`.  The corrected morphic theorem
   194	applies because the actual `g1:A2->U` is everywhere defined and dominant;
   195	its full boundary dual **multigraph** is a forest.
   196	
   197	Let `R_1,...,R_c` be the irreducible components of `R`.  Let `Sigma` be the
   198	finite set of intrinsic affine singular points at which the normalization
   199	has at least two preimages, and put
   200	
   201	```text
   202	r_p=#nu^(-1)(p),                   p in Sigma.
   203	```
   204	
   205	Form the affine incidence multigraph `Gamma_R`: its vertices are the `c`
   206	normalization components and the points of `Sigma`, with one edge for every
   207	branch over every `p`.  This graph is a minor, up to harmless edge
   208	subdivision, of the resolved SNC boundary graph:
   209	
   210	- over a singular point of the normal surface `Y`, the resolution fibre is
   211	  connected and lies wholly in the boundary;
   212	- embedded resolution separates all local branches;
   213	- contract each connected exceptional cluster over `p` to the corresponding
   214	  incidence vertex;
   215	- delete infinity vertices and unibranch exceptional twigs.
   216	
   217	A minor or subdivision of a forest is a forest.  Parallel branch edges are
   218	retained; in particular, a self-node would already make a two-edge cycle.
   219	
   220	Normalization additivity for compactly supported Euler characteristic gives
   221	
   222	```text
   223	e_c(R)=sum_i e_c(A1)-sum_(p in Sigma)(r_p-1)
   224	      =c-sum_p(r_p-1).                                 (5.1)
   225	```
   226	
   227	Write `s=|Sigma|`, `E=sum_p r_p`, and let `h` be the number of connected
   228	components of `Gamma_R`.  Since `Gamma_R` is a forest,
   229	
   230	```text
   231	E=(c+s)-h.
   232	```
   233	
   234	Substitution into (5.1) yields
   235	
   236	```text
   237	e_c(R)=c-E+s=h=b0(R).                                  (5.2)
   238	```
   239	
   240	Together with (4.1), this proves (0.1).  Notice that connectedness of a
   241	projective target discriminant was never assumed.  The exact answer is the
   242	number of connected components of the **affine reduced branch**.
   243	
   244	## 6. Euler closure of the projective-base branch
   245	
   246	The frozen degree-three fibre census is
   247	
   248	```text
   249	partition (1,1,1): z outside B, u(z)=3;
   250	partition (2,1):   z in B minus S0, u(z)=1;
   251	partition (3):     z in S0, u(z)=0.
   252	```
   253	
   254	The fixed-sheet theorem makes `S0` finite.  Euler integration gives
   255	
   256	```text
   257	e(U)=3-2e(B)-|S0|.                                     (6.1)
   258	```
   259	
   260	The ruling gives
   261	
   262	```text
   263	e(U)=e(C)+Q,       Q=sum_t(q_t-1)>=0.                  (6.2)
   264	```
   265	
   266	Insert `e(B)=b0(B)>=1`.  If `C=P1`, (6.1)--(6.2) demand
   267	
   268	```text
   269	2b0(B)+|S0|+Q=1,
   270	```
   271	
   272	whose left side is at least two.  Thus `C=P1` is impossible.  If `C=A1`,
   273	the same equations demand
   274	
   275	```text
   276	2b0(B)+|S0|+Q=2.
   277	```
   278	
   279	All terms are nonnegative integers and `b0(B)>=1`, so the unique solution is
   280	
   281	```text
   282	b0(B)=1,        |S0|=0,        Q=0,        e(U)=1.     (6.3)
   283	```
   284	
   285	Since each `q_t>=1`, `Q=0` means every reduced fibre of the ruling consists
   286	of one affine line.  Equation `S0=empty` means every target point has at
   287	least one unramified block sheet; it does not make `g2|U` finite flat of
   288	degree three over the branch.
   289	
   290	## 7. Projective infinity and discriminant firewall
   291	
   292	The object in (0.1) is the reduced affine curve `B=g2(R)_red`.  Its ordinary
   293	projective closure `Bbar` cannot contain the line at infinity as an
   294	irreducible component: every component of `Bbar` is the closure of an affine
   295	component of `B`.  Chau's Corollary 2 says set-theoretically
   296	
   297	```text
   298	|Bbar intersect L_infinity|=1,
   299	```
   300	
   301	because `B` is a nonempty union of components of the whole `A_F`.
   302	
   303	A different object can occur in a chosen class-`(3,3)` homogenized
   304	binary-cubic presentation: its homogeneous discriminant divisor may acquire
   305	`L_infinity` as a component when the leading binary cubic is everywhere
   306	singular along infinity.  In that case the homogeneous divisor is
   307	
   308	```text
   309	L_infinity union Bbar
   310	```
   311	
   312	set-theoretically, possibly with multiplicities.  The extra line is not part
   313	of affine `B`, is not charged in `e(B)`, and need not be the closure of any
   314	affine source-ramification component.  If no such line component occurs, the
   315	homogeneous reduced discriminant agrees with `Bbar` on support.  The proof of
   316	Sections 3--6 is deliberately independent of both cases, of discriminant
   317	multiplicities, and of projective basepoints.
   318	
   319	## 8. Controls and exact scope
   320	
   321	Three controls mark the load-bearing hypotheses.
   322	
   323	1. A smooth `C*` boundary component has a one-vertex forest but Euler number
   324	   zero.  Thus rationality plus forest is insufficient without the
