    60	the finite set of values with no unramified block sheet, and the Euler strata
    61	of sheets lost at infinity by `g1`.  No current promoted theorem bounds these
    62	quantities with the sign needed to eliminate `C=P1`.
    63	
    64	## 1. Objects kept separate
    65	
    66	Use compactly supported topological Euler characteristic, which is additive
    67	and satisfies constructible Fubini for complex algebraic maps.  The relevant
    68	objects are:
    69	
    70	```text
    71	R       = source non-etale support in Y;
    72	B       = g2(R), the reduced target branch/discriminant support;
    73	g2^-1(B)= full inverse image, including possible unramified sheets;
    74	U       = Y minus R;
    75	W       = g2(U), an open subset of the target A2;
    76	V       = g1(A2), an open subset of U;
    77	M       = U minus V, the first-leg nonproperness residue.
    78	```
    79	
    80	Neither `U` nor `V` is obtained by deleting the full inverse image of `B`.
    81	An unramified point of `g2` can lie over a branch value.  Likewise `g1` is not
    82	assumed proper or surjective onto `U`, and the ruling `rho` is not assumed to
    83	be a coordinate on the source `A2`.
    84	
    85	The promoted fixed-sheet theorem says that every irreducible target divisor
    86	has a generically unramified component upstairs.  Since `g2|U` is etale and
    87	therefore open, it follows that
    88	
    89	```text
    90	S0=A2 minus W
    91	```
    92	
    93	has codimension at least two, hence is finite.  This is the cover-side content
    94	of the constant-unit obstruction.  It is stronger than merely saying that
    95	`B` is nonempty and is already charged by the promoted Galois-block packet.
    96	
    97	## 2. Finite-flat source/target ledger
    98	
    99	Choose a finite constructible stratification `{S_alpha}` of the target such
   100	that the finite map is topologically trivial and all fibre data below are
   101	constant on each stratum.  For `z in S_alpha`, define
   102	
   103	```text
   104	u_alpha = # (g2^-1(z) intersect U),
   105	r_alpha = # (g2^-1(z) intersect R_red),
   106	delta_alpha = sum_(y in R_z)(length_y(Y_z)-1).           (2.1)
   107	```
   108	
   109	All residue fields are `C`.  Finite flatness gives a fibre of scheme length
   110	`d2`, while every point in `U` has length one.  Therefore
   111	
   112	```text
   113	d2=u_alpha+r_alpha+delta_alpha,
   114	d2-u_alpha=r_alpha+delta_alpha.                         (2.2)
   115	```
   116	
   117	This is the exact distinction between deleting the source support and
   118	charging the higher scheme length at its points.  Constructible Fubini gives
   119	
   120	```text
   121	e(U)=sum_alpha u_alpha e(S_alpha),                       (2.3)
   122	e(R_red)=sum_alpha r_alpha e(S_alpha),
   123	e(Y)=sum_alpha (u_alpha+r_alpha)e(S_alpha).
   124	```
   125	
   126	Off `B`, `u_alpha=d2`.  Since `e(A2)=1`, put
   127	
   128	```text
   129	D2=sum_(S_alpha subset B)(d2-u_alpha)e(S_alpha).
   130	```
   131	
   132	Then
   133	
   134	```text
   135	e(U)=d2-D2.                                             (2.4)
   136	```
   137	
   138	Formula (2.4) is exact, but `D2` is not a nonnegative integer count.  Its
   139	coefficient `d2-u_alpha` is pointwise nonnegative, yet the Euler number of a
   140	curve stratum may be zero or negative.  Thus finite flatness, the degree and
   141	the generic inertia partitions do not by themselves give an upper or lower
   142	bound on `D2`.
   143	
   144	The fixed-sheet theorem says that on every one-dimensional branch stratum
   145	
   146	```text
   147	1<=u_alpha<=d2-2.                                       (2.5)
   148	```
   149	
   150	The upper bound uses the scheme length at least two of one ramified point.
   151	It recovers `d2>=3`; the stronger no-two-block theorem is already promoted by
   152	the Galois packet.
   153	
   154	## 3. First-leg sheet-loss ledger
   155	
   156	Choose a finite constructible stratification `{T_beta}` of `U` on which
   157	
   158	```text
   159	a_beta=#g1^-1(y),             y in T_beta,
   160	```
   161	
   162	is constant.  Quasi-finiteness and the normalization lemma in the promoted
   163	block theorem give
   164	
   165	```text
   166	0<=a_beta<=d1,
   167	```
   168	
   169	with generic value `d1`; the strata with `a_beta=0` are precisely `M=U\V`.
   170	Constructible Fubini for `g1` gives
   171	
   172	```text
   173	1=e(A2)=sum_beta a_beta e(T_beta)
   174	       =d1 e(U)-D1,                                    (3.1)
   175	
   176	D1=sum_beta(d1-a_beta)e(T_beta)=d1 e(U)-1.              (3.2)
   177	```
   178	
   179	Again `D1` is a signed Euler integral, not the cardinality or degree of the
   180	nonproperness locus.  In particular, `d1>=2` supplies no sign-improving bound
   181	beyond the exact equality (3.2).
   182	
   183	Let `f(z)=#F^-1(z)` for the full Keller map `F=g2 g1`.  Pointwise,
   184	
   185	```text
   186	f(z)=sum_(y in U_z) a(y),
   187	
   188	d1d2-f(z)
   189	 =d1(d2-u(z))+sum_(y in U_z)(d1-a(y)).                  (3.3)
   190	```
   191	
   192	Euler integration of (3.3) gives only
   193	
   194	```text
   195	d1d2-1=d1(d2-e(U))+(d1e(U)-1),                          (3.4)
   196	```
   197	
   198	an algebraic identity.  It is invalid to count the target discriminant once,
   199	then count the full inverse image or the first-leg missing sheets as a second
   200	independent positive defect.  Formula (3.3) is the exact non-double-counting
   201	rule.
   202	
   203	## 4. Comparison with the ruling
   204	
   205	The promoted ruling theorem gives
   206	
   207	```text
   208	e(U)=e(C)+Q,            Q=sum_t(q_t-1)>=0,               (4.1)
   209	```
   210	
   211	with `e(C)=1` for `A1` and `e(C)=2` for `P1`.  Combining Sections 2--3,
   212	
   213	```text
   214	D2=d2-e(C)-Q,
   215	D1=d1(e(C)+Q)-1.                                        (4.2)
   216	```
   217	
   218	Thus the two branches require
   219	
   220	```text
   221	C=A1:  D2=d2-1-Q,       D1=d1(1+Q)-1;
   222	C=P1:  D2=d2-2-Q,       D1=d1(2+Q)-1.                   (4.3)
   223	```
   224	
   225	The projective-base branch forces a large first-leg sheet-loss integral
   226	`D1>=2d1-1`, but no promoted theorem bounds that integral from above.  The
   227	finite-cover integral `D2` can have either sign.  Consequently (4.3) is a
   228	precise threat ledger, not a contradiction.
   229	
   230	## 5. Cubic specialization
   231	
   232	Let `d2=3`.  At a branch value, any non-etale fibre point has scheme length
   233	at least two.  Hence there is at most one unramified point.  The fixed-sheet
   234	theorem makes the generic branch partition exactly `(2,1)`.  Since
   235	`S0=A2\W` is finite, one has
   236	
   237	```text
   238	u(z)=3,   z outside B;
   239	u(z)=1,   z in B minus S0;
   240	u(z)=0,   z in S0.                                      (5.1)
   241	```
   242	
   243	No smoothness or irreducibility of `B` is needed.  Additivity gives
   244	
   245	```text
   246	e(U)=3e(A2 minus B)+e(B minus S0)
   247	    =3-2e(B)-|S0|.                                      (5.2)
   248	```
   249	
   250	Comparison with (4.1) yields the exact alternatives
   251	
   252	```text
   253	C=A1:  2e(B)+|S0|+Q=2;
   254	C=P1:  2e(B)+|S0|+Q=1.                                 (5.3)
   255	```
   256	
   257	For `C=P1`, `|S0|+Q` must be odd and
   258	`e(B)=(1-|S0|-Q)/2`.  This is useful finite arithmetic.  It does not exclude
   259	the branch, but it forces `e(B)<=0`; any independent proof that the actual
   260	cubic branch support has positive Euler number would close `P1`.  One minimal
   261	numerical pattern is
   262	
   263	```text
   264	e(B)=0,                     |S0|=1,                    Q=0.  (5.4)
   265	```
   266	
   267	Eliminating `P1` therefore requires an independent restriction on the actual
   268	cubic discriminant topology, on totally ramified target values, or on ruling
   269	fibre splitting.
   270	
   271	## 6. Controls and counterexamples to naive bounds
   272	
   273	### 6.1 Fixed degree three has unbounded cover-side Euler ledgers
   274	
   275	Put `p(s)=s^3-3s`.  For a polynomial `phi(x,y)`, let
   276	
   277	```text
   278	Y_phi={p(s)=phi(x,y)} subset A3,
   279	g_phi:Y_phi->A2_(x,y).                                  (6.1)
   280	```
   281	
   282	This is monic finite flat of degree three.  Its source ramification is
   283	`s=1` or `s=-1`, and the two target branch curves are
   284	`phi=-2` and `phi=2`.  Each branch fibre has one double ramified root and one
   285	unramified simple root, so `g_phi(U_phi)=A2` and `S0` is empty.
   286	
   287	For odd primes `N>=5`, take first `phi=x^N`.  The surface is smooth: a
   288	singular point would require `s=+-1`, `x=0`, and `p(s)=0`, which is
   289	impossible.  It is integral; the pole valuation of `x^N` rules out a root of
   290	the cubic in `C(x,y)` when `3` does not divide `N`.  The branch support is a
   291	disjoint union of `2N` affine lines, so
   292	
   293	```text
   294	e(U_phi)=3-4N.                                          (6.2)
   295	```
   296	
   297	For `phi=y^2+x^N`, the same Jacobian check gives a smooth surface; the pole
   298	valuation at `y=infinity` has value `-2`, which is not divisible by three,
   299	and rules out a rational root of the cubic, so the surface is integral.
   300	Each of the two branch curves is a smooth affine hyperelliptic curve with
   301	Euler number `2-N`; hence
   302	
   303	```text
   304	e(U_phi)=3-2(4-2N)=4N-5.                                (6.3)
   305	```
   306	
   307	Thus `e(U)` is unbounded in both directions at fixed `d2=3`, even for smooth
   308	connected finite-flat covers whose generic branch inertia has a fixed sheet
   309	and whose etale-source image is all of `A2`.
   310	
   311	These are not block controls.  On `U_phi`, the different
   312	`3(s^2-1)` is a nonconstant unit, contradicting the promoted condition
   313	`O(U)^*=C^*`; no first leg is supplied.  Their exact role is to disprove any
   314	attempt to assign a sign to `D2` using only degree, flatness and generic
   315	ramification type.
   316	
   317	### 6.2 The missing-point term is real
   318	
   319	The universal monic cubic
   320	
   321	```text
   322	g_c:A2_(s,a)->A2_(a,b),              b=-s^3-as          (6.4)
   323	```
   324	
   325	has ramification parabola `a=-3s^2` and cuspidal target branch
   326	
   327	```text
   328	4a^3+27b^2=0.
   329	```
   330	
   331	Away from the cusp, a simple unramified sheet survives.  Over `(0,0)` the
   332	fibre is `s^3=0`, so no point of `U` remains.  Therefore
   333	
   334	```text
   335	e(B)=1,                 |S0|=1,                 e(U)=0,
   336	```
   337	
   338	exactly as (5.2) predicts.  This control prevents silently replacing `W` by
   339	all of the target.
   340	
   341	### 6.3 The complete-base qualitative control
   342	
   343	Let
   344	
   345	```text
   346	U0=(P1 x P1) minus diagonal.
   347	```
   348	
   349	The diagonal is ample, so `U0` is smooth affine and rational.  Projection to
   350	either factor is an `A1`-fibration over `P1`, every fibre is `A1`, and
   351	
   352	```text
   353	e(U0)=4-2=2,                O(U0)^*=C^*.
   354	```
   355	
   356	The unit statement follows because a unit divisor would be supported on the
   357	single diagonal, whose nonzero class is not principal.  The completion
   358	boundary is one rational vertex, hence a forest.
   359	
   360	In affine-quadric coordinates
   361	
   362	```text
   363	U0={b^2-4ac=1} subset A3,
   364	```
   365	
   366	the formulas
   367	
   368	```text
   369	a=x,        b=1+2xy,        c=y(1+xy)                   (6.5)
   370	```
   371	
   372	give an etale open embedding `A2->U0`; its complement is the line
   373	`{a=0,b=-1}`.  On its image the inverse is covered by
   374	`y=(b-1)/(2a)` when `a!=0` and `y=2c/(b+1)` when `b+1!=0`.
   375	This realizes the first-leg topology only at generic degree
   376	one.  It neither supplies `d1>=2` nor realizes `U0` as the maximal etale open
   377	of the promoted non-Galois finite-flat block.  Constructing the missing
   378	simultaneous data would be comparable to the campaign's core difficulty.
   379	
   380	## 7. Maximum-safe conclusion and next discriminator
   381	
   382	The exact transferable result is the pair of constructible ledgers
   383	(2.4), (3.2), their non-double-counting identity (3.3), and the cubic closure
   384	(5.2).  They improve the qualitative bound to a finite arithmetic gate once
   385	`e(B)`, `S0` and the ruling excess `Q` are known.  They do not currently rule
   386	out `C=P1`.
   387	
   388	The cheapest decisive successors are:
   389	
   390	1. In a fixed cubic presentation, compute the normalization Euler number of
   391	   `B`, the totally ramified set `S0`, and `Q`.  Equation (5.3) then decides
   392	   the base branch without any coordinate assumption on the ruling.
   393	2. Normalize `U` in `C(x,y)` and stratify its boundary
   394	   `Z minus A2`.  Any geometric upper bound on
   395	   `D1=sum(d1-a_beta)e(T_beta)` below `2d1-1` eliminates `P1`.
   396	3. If external nonproperness-curve theorems are imported later, apply them
   397	   separately to `B subset A(F)` and to the first-leg image residue.  Do not
   398	   identify these curves or count their Euler defects twice.
   399	
   400	Until one of these data is charged, `C=P1` remains genuinely open.
